        <p>Although the mvn .1.1 -ervoe in the It iyat Mausoinsini at Frnyhore on the 14,11 included Sir Arthur Sullivan's new enthem, its duration we. under three quarters of an hour. So may readily be imagined, this and, hut very beautif oat service is • most painful one to the Queen end bee itmily alwaye endeavour to ptrsrade the Sovereign to curtail If. est far posisible. All parts of Frogronre have a groat int, teat for the Queen. She purchased it nut of the Privy puree In 1841. The inventory wan mede by Mr. Thomas Dowhiggen, who was employed by the Woods and Forest., and the valuetion is put down at £3002 10i. The Imine itself is damp, and unhealthy. In tile grountin, bssid. the Mau- .leum, and the gothic ruin chosignel by Sir Osioffrey Wigathville, Is the Queen., fess House. '[iii. is • cur. , lons inert of conical shaped bungalow, .formed of wood, planter. and fancy red tit., and j insert open. I roofed pamege, to the servant's bowie, which is of the ' eame drsivs. Frequently,when at W indsor,the Queen breakfasts in , the Tea House,tha silver nervier. and tent boin t brought in • two honed /Mayon. the watt e being .Irawn from a little peliehed grergranite fountain close by, and the milk fetched from the dairy opposite, which is guarded by two Scotchwomen. The Quern is • great tea drinker. Two piper, perform during her klejeety-ii breakfast, and when she settle, down to her whrk, her little grandchildren play about,flnding great pleantre in • seeviaw, which is arranged for them clone by. In the early day. of h, wid..whood,thel.2.l,ll selectel • eite,close to the Proem.° Mausoleum for • miniature plantation, and the first tr,e wan plant.,l about twelve montha afttet the death of the Prince Coneort,by the late Ring a the Retitle.. The Printers of Wales planted there,bout twenty.two yeses fy, • c.nk tiro, which in now in excellent condition, and no numerous have been this contritmtions 'ince then, that it would indeed be difficult to lay who among Royal and distinguished lks has not,. 010 time or another, planted • tree of some description in this particular plantation. In the veseaainn of the Vented singers and the beet chorus singing, we of the British I.len, undoubtedly rank first. And according to Sir Arthur Sullivan, Great Britain is now the most appreciative musical nation in the world, Everywhere, he says, you find trio 'pie who know how to pl., and how to xing. and what is more important, people who know how to listen. Speaking of Sir Arthur Sulliven,it may intoreat many of my readers to know how file I...lCC:mid," perhaps , the most succeesful none °, m tern times, was written. One night while watching et the bedaide of. inch brother who had fallen into • peaceful sleep. Sir Arthur ' happened to come micron Nome verses nf Adelaide Peon- toes. In the stillness of the night he read them many times, and as he did so he conceived their musical equi- valent. A sheet of music paper wheat hand and he be- gan to write. In • sh.xt tune the sang was complete, and nut long afterwards in the publiener's hands. The decoration of our homes and churches, in in full swing. Doily and mistletoe abiund on eeeee side, and few householders at the preeent moment fail to exhibit •ntne little sign of the festive mason, so near at heed. Most pt,ple if Raked where rn at letoe grows would answer —on the oak—but an • matter of fact the apple, the hawthorn,the pear treecand ethers of the seine h ttani. cal specie., are its principal supporters. The oak drier not bike kindly to the paraditic plant, and for one breach taken from the tough bark tithe monarch of the wood., a dor, are ennualy cut from those trees have inentioned, Traveller. in N,orth-Weetern France during the winter season, can hardly have failed to notice ens, luxuriant growth of the mieth toe In the gm. apple orchards of Normandy and Brittany. These are our chief enure..s of supply. Held in high eateem hp the •ncieut Druid, awl atilt occupying the place of honor in our houses at Chriat- mite, it is remarkable that the mielet • has alwaya been tat-toned in connection with thy Church Never hat It been permitted • place amid the decorationa of the sacred edifice And equally rigor,oa been the rule of the past, in reference to architertUral ornarneqt, While the nab is 'routines,t eeeee where, sod the hilly vine, ivy, nnd other plant. are constantly recurring,. the oculptore and carving with which our early church.. are adorned, the ;Valeta is everywhere con.picuoun be it, &amp;been,. The reason seems to he, that in the minds of the mediseval Churchmen, it was associated with heathenish form of mythology, or wan saggeatiee of usage, which weee regarded as profane, There see no he.. than 15,000 plants which •re opera• ally eeniceable to mankind ! Over 5,0011 have been roughly clawed a. ist,nomic end food plants. About 300 have fibre. that are of use In the textile ars, term 88 different plants potash. webs, sod Odine have town ex- tracted, and 650 are nf use ti the dyer, and to make ink Tannin a preaent iaeounilc,n plant., while ...inn and ba.smie gums are Melded by not leen than 393. Tilers are ten wax tree. not elrobr, and 330 which have been made to yield reeential iOn Fortrdmir, ot more. are reed in making paver, r, ha, beets utilised In makiag rooting prep...on, and 740 have bees turned to accmint ranicellanneta building imeratioos. 01 all plant. known, only 815 are known to be in any deirrce polannntre.</p>
