        <p>turns reund even before the as • friend. You punish and commend the iptilt3 total result of all this Unioe. the political development o , been delayed for at feint a BIMLAIN posed as the friend he has been the means of, them over to thou enemies. he represents ore" suspect r</p>
        <p>South Africa, and the beet thing our bungling Government eau do is to !nay° South Africa rigidly alone.</p>
        <p>But, after all, it is now becoming plainer every day that the most colossal blunder which the Government has made is in connection with the Indian frontier. In spire of all warnings and of the example set them by their predecessors, they persisted in breaking a definite pledge, and when tho frontiersmen resented this breach of faith they sent one of the largest British armies that has ever been equipped into their country. But what is the revolt The tribeernon have fought with extraordinary skill and bravery and have inflicted heavy losses on the invading force. Although faith the English and native soldiers have displayed prodigies of valour and en- durance, they have been compelled to retreat by the advent of winter without aecomplishing the object in view. This retreat will ho represented all through Asia as a British reverse, and may poesibly he the means of inciting extremely seri- ous outbreaks of Mahornotan fanaticism in India !and elsewhere. It certainly cannot under any circumstances lead to an increase of British pres- tige. All round, therefore, this Government has boon onfortimate or iir'nmpetent. The record of its two or three years of office is of a depressing charactor, and does not inspire one with much confidence in the future.</p>
        <p>THERE is now a Mendedly impreved An i raprnirril outlook in connection with the outlook. lock-out of engineers. The question . of acceptiog the terms laid diem by the Master's Federation was submitted to the whole of th members of the societies affected by the dispute with the result that they have been rejected by enormous majorities. In the engineers society no fewer than 53,413 men voted against the terms,. while only 530 voted for their acceptance. This Ipoll seems to have convinced the employers that if I they insisted upon their conditione, there could be I no settlement for a long time to come, and they appear therefore to have resumed the conference this week with a desire to find a way out of the I deadlm.k if possible. Consequently, it is now hoped than an agreement may be arrived at before many days are over. During the week souse rather ' remarkable statements have been made about the I general sithject if capital versus trade. unionism. '1 Politicians of different parties, heads of colleges, I. and even employers of labour have pronounced very emphatically against " union-smashing " or' any attempt to destroy the legitimate operations I, of trades unionism. Probably the most significant of these utterances is to he found in a short speech by Mr. Piaatz, the Lord Mayor of Belfast. Mr, Pinero is a leading member of the great firm of Belfast shipbuilders, Harlan and Wolff, and wlmt he Bays should, I therefore, carry the utmost weight. In effect, his speech is a decided rebuff for the more militant members of the Masters' Federation. Ile is not alarmed by foreign competition, and he gives British workinen credit for being the most competeut and best trained iti the world. What is needed. in his opinion, is not the breaking down . of organisations which have raised the competency of workmen,but the providing by employers of the best mechanical contrivauces suitable If their , work. In other words, in the opinion of this re- I presentative of a conspicuously successful indus- trial establishment, the power to combat foreign competition lim with the employers sad not with ' the men. to</p>
