        <head>EXA</head>
        <p>THE EXA</p>
        <p>DV.CEMBEIt. 170, 1897.</p>
        <p>As the end of the year approaches people almost insti.tiyely begin to be retrospective and to" take stock," so to speak, of past events. In fact, in the political world retrospection seems to be the order of the day at the present tune. The sorry con- trast between the promises and the performances of the Government that is now in power is no doubt one of the main caums of this tendency to look backwards,especially on the part of its critics. Yet a survey of the itehievoinents of the present Action, istration, especially in foreign and colonial mat- ters, is not calculated to Aldo,' patriotic Britons any special gratification. Tan promise that this powerful Unionist Government would raise British prestige to the higlimt pitch has been strangely and woefully falsified. This is very keenly realised when one conies to c mtemplato the changes that have some over the scene in three different quarteni of the world—the East of Europe, Booth Africa, and India Take the East of Europe first. Two or three years ago the oppreesed peoples in that part of the world locked to England more than to any other European country for the relief which they greatly needed each which they were entitled to expect. They knew that in the past this country had en more than occasion given stilistontial help to struggling aationalitim, and they knew, too, that we were under very special treaty obligations to throw a shield of protection over the Christians of Asia Minor. It was never for a moment imagined that England would shrink, through sheer weakness, froin purl mining it. part , in the great Eastern drama. Yet what is the position of affairs now ? From being one of the strongest and most decisive members of the Con- cert of Enrope, Great Britain has, owing to the Want of vigour or the want of nerve of its I foreign Secretary, fallen Irons the topmost to the , bottommost place. The Eastern quation is not yet settled. The Armeniane are still being ex- terminated, partly by silence and partly by slow starvation, and Crete is still in pi state of anarchy, but noone now looks to this country to take • Issuing part in the beneficent work of settlement.</p>
        <p>All the other members of (ho Concert of Europe appear by tarn to kilm a,, initiativo of one kind or another, but the rote of this csuntry has boon re- duced to that id X Iminhle follower. It is true that Lord 5A1.'91811,1 does rum:Aimee mildly pro- test or feebly hesitotte when proposals are math, by the other nationa, bat ho invariably yields M tho end. In connection with the Eastern quesCon, therefore, no consolation is to he derived from a contemplation of the history of the last two years. Now, let tie him to South Africa. It would be unfair to say that everything was goikg smoothly in that portion of the British dominions at toe time of tho G niernment's acces- sion to yerwer, Init, it csn bo said that the condition of things is much worse n•wir than it was then, and that this change for the -worse is mostly use to I loverninetital mistusnago- monk A couple of pure ago there was a con- oiderablo amount of discontent amongst the "Outlanders " id the Transvaal Itopublic, but OM was On tho other hand a steady drift of opinion and ildbionco in favour of the redress of grievance.. The large Dutch popirlation in Cape Colony and other parts of South Africa was more and more lisp wed to support the &amp;mends of the disfranchised ,),ktlanklors, and those demands would no iloiMt hare toes gradually conceded. Then came the JAlntiOn raid, and it is not too much to soy that the miemanagoinent by the authco itiea of the curious nneetions raiusl by that</p>
        <p>lawless enterprise hasltsl to the most demi-OmM.,</p>
        <p>result,. South Africa has almost been. drivun I into civil war, the Dutch iviirtion of the community, who for., on it romem- bcreit a maj,irity, have had their faith in tb.intcgvity of the Imporial (foyarninent wavy rudaly shake. Mr 11011dOiMl i• the looter</p>
