        <p>WIN W 'CR.</p>
        <p>It will he sewn that a photographic liver of Win wick Church adorns the eottst almansck which we present to our readers to-day. The spire of this ancient church iv. familiar object to all who live in Warrington and district. Standing on an ele- vated position it overlooks Warrington in the val. ley below. The •illage itself in pleimetly situated, and ha. a quiet, old-won't air about It. In summer it is greatly resorted to by cychste from • distance, and in our illustration two cyclists are to be seen near the old-fashioned walla. The photographer has also includeit in his plate. hansom cab, but the church carries the mind back to times long before the advent of cycles, or before Mr. Hansom in- vented his now familiar vehicle. Btandlog beneath the shades of the tower, the visitor look. •pon ntaannry which has weathered the storms of , more than four centuries. Could r he... walls but</p>
        <p>speak,what an interesting ,hapter of history should we br able to hear ! There is perhaps a iittle irony in the tact that this historic village of Winwick is ehortly to be the home of • large number of idiot. , and lunatics. Already the trowel of the bricksetter is to be heard in the district, nod within the next !few years a pile of buildings will base been erected It 4 Cost of over. quarter of. • million of moony to provide accommodation for the increasing number of persons afflicted with imbecility. ! The eymology of Winwick is Bowel.' mythical. According t• the l!'treed Usher, Winwick was :lam Oweniquic, one of the twenty cities collected by Odd.. out of Nennius, and it. modern name he thought was huts corruption of the British original. But Winwick more probably owes its name to the Bacons than the Briton., and like Wigan, its name (Winwick) mean.. place of light au I victdry, and commemorate. some battle once fought there There is another place of the same name in No-th- : aroptonehire, which was given in 143 by Bilward the Confess ir to the abbey of the Virgin and nt. , Peter in Coventry, the name of which, like that of</p>
