        <head>COU RT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>COU RT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Ihree performance. were given in the Col rt Theatre, W•rtington, last week by the hi arr. ington Amateur Dramatic Society, with the laudable object of aiding the War- ' rington Infirmary. The So'ciety way founded in 1801 and during the 36 years since that date many performances of a rehlly high claei have been rub. mined to the Warrington public by the members, generally far the furtherance of some charitable iito (eel. Of late the strength of the Society has been seriously impaired by the inroads of death, and from other elitism but the performances of the pre- Sent week have revealed the fact that the talent of the younger members of the Society is of • higher onier than was antici- pated. Consequently the old supporters of the Dramatic Society feel exceedingly gratified that --though the loosen caused by death can never be compensated for—there arc younger aqui, ants to dramatic honours who can and will up- hold the status of the Society. Mr. C.H. ilautrey'n farcical comedy, "The Private Secretary,' and the comediett• " My Lady Help," have been the pieces de reristuncr which have taxed the powers of the Sete.), during the last three nights. The inanevo of tile theatre on each occasion is a splendid testimony to the reputation of the So- ciety, and the Sustained enthusiasm of the au lieneo ion proof -if proof were needed—that the love of amateur dratnevice is rather on the increase than on the wane, locally. While there is nothing to find fault with in the acting of either of the comedies, we think that it would have b-en judicious Ifs selection hail been made of pierce rather more dis- similar than 'Thin ['neat° Secretary," and '' Led-) Help." Each haa features very much in common -in fac" one might almost betook,' upon as a larger edition of the other—and en effective contract could have been acceptably afforded by opening the pri ceedings with some really dramatic sketch. Ar it way, three hours and a half of con- tinuous merriment way a big call on physical en- durance My Lady Help " was preeented in the ) alf hour from 7.30 to 8 p.m., the characters being taken as follows ;--Jack Desborough (an artist), ME ilfulY Mosley Lady Eva Deyborough (his wife), Min Lucie Wenharn end Benjamin Pennygrahs, (his unc)e), Mr. T. J. Hulse. ihe conversion of the uncle to a common-senen understanding of his nephew's marriage to • " stuck-up "aristocrat, was prettily pourtrayed, the artiste, claiming equal commendation. homer hing like three hours were occupied in producineThe Pri•ate Secretary,"but no portion ol the period could be termed in the least monotonous. The cast was as followe—Mr. Marsland, M.F.11., Mr, Arthur Browne ; Harry Slarsland, (hie nephew), Mr. Sidney Wellhead Mr. Cattermole, Mr. Alfred Wood ; Douglas Cattermole (Ids nephew), Mr. Chart. H. Wood; Rev. Robert Spalding, Mr. Stanley Wright ; Mr. Sidney (Dimon (Tailor of Bond-street), Mr. Harry Mosley John (a Servant), Mr. Brough- ell ; Koos (a Writ Server), Mr. Roger Pierpoi•t ; Hardener, Master Ernest A. Wood.; Edith Mareland (Daughter to Mr. Maryland), Mice Katie Brundrett; Eva Webster (her friend 111111 i companion), Miss May Pierpoint ; Mrs. Stead (Donnish Landlady), Mrs. James Fairclough; Miss Ashford, lire. /Cowley. It is hardly necessary ri state that three acts are comprised in the play, viz., Act I. (Douglas Cattersoole y Chamber.), " Irouna”; Act 1, (Mr. Marsland's Country Small," Kul Cry" ; £cl3, (Mr. Maryland's Country Seat), Run to Earth " ; but we are called open to any. that the scenic presentment of them was very tasteful In- deed. (tin no light task to give credit where credit is due ' in this particular instance, any I he . participants in the ch•rening corned...its acquitted : themselves with honour. It distinction unlyt be mate, then Mrs Fairclough, Mr. Ailred Woods. Mr Stanley Wright, and Mr. C. 11. Wood hest came up to our idea of what acting should be. lire. Fair' cough in particular was strikingly at ease and natural in her isle as a well-to-do landlady, and fully merited the hearty appleume which ha- accorded taef. in Mr. Stanley Wright the society have found a young artiste with histrionic abilities which only need developing to make him a most valuable addition to their strength. His impersonation of the trying part of the Rev. Robert Spalding --whose child-like tomplicity and nino. °once, combined with the oddest of mannerisme makes his personality a difficult one to pourtray-- war clever in the eitreirie, and betokened studious</p>
        <p>attrution to the important details of the part. The contretemps into which he le placed, and the harsh rebuffs be recover from Mr. Cetera.°le were tree-</p>
        <p>mottbly mirth-provoking. In the character of M llattermole we fled Mr. Allred Woods lode at bin beat. The comicality and boisteromnem of the part , were moot Iturnorouoly interpreted by him, and he was undoubtedly • great "draw.' A joint work fell upon the shoulders of Mr. U.II.Wood, but he proved fully "pal to the task of being Mr. Getter- mile.n nephew and privath secretary at one and the same time. A capital Pitmen, :he tailor. with tendencies to "soaring to the upper crust of society,. was found in Mr. Mosley. Ile acted his part to perfection. Mr. Arthur Browns,' and Sir. Sydney Wall-red were eery ouccemlul in their respective character., though they did not, perhaps ,show the mime artistic sifts taut. gentle- men just mentioned. The two girl-companions in the omit were charmingly repreoented by Mies I Katie Brundrott and Mime May Pierpoint. The former had rather the mom to to, and did it well, I but etch appeared quite at homo en her part. Praise must not be stinted in referring to Sire. Bewley, who very cleierly chaperoned the two girls mid ' appeared to pmts....vim m a lady with epintualistiC tenifencim highly developet The minor parts takeu by Messrs. Broughall. iv ?torpidity and Krnmt it. Woods were g set Add vi the talented acting. the comerest of "make-ups," and we cannot but clam the performance an cis it 'ending very highly ilk deed to the credit of the Dramatic . ociety. _ . . .</p>
