        <p>Sir,—l read with sympathetic interest the plain spoken opinion. expressed in this camn at week hy correspondent who wrote aline, the wee/ling hande. plume of t' A Uncoated Member." It may be • eatU- faction to him Cc know that he is not the only disgusted member, and that he it far frem alone in hi. opinion.. This Mann particialwly the Warringb,n Rugby Vootball ChM has laid itself open to criticism. not by its actions exactly, but rather be It. inaction. lo start at the be- ginning,l twat say that I don't think the prment season ww entered upon by tho committee with any real in- tention of trying for the Lacrashir, championehip. trite the Club sin the League and presumably as much In earnest in its endayouts as other clubs, but as so actinl fact there never was any chance if the present team carrying .ff the honourr. The committee made no organised or determined effort at acconipti.hing what the teem I. kept going for—to beat all opponents and secure the cha mpionnlrip trophy. In contrset to this lackadaisical, truut.todurk sort of policy we find elute like Oldham, Swinton, Salford and Widos strengthening their ranks IT every possible means and entering the competition with fired idea wd purr,iss of winning the charnpinwhip. They haw imported playem right and lett by means they themselves only know—but certainly moans within the power of War- rington Club, and consequently they have recorded vic- tory upon victory and are now near [hobo', of the league table. Thew are some of the grounds on which I blame We Warrington Cummittee. They must know that both the present teame,though • good average lot and credit- able a being entirely liseaLare totally incapable of sac cesilulip combiting their strongest opponents with any. thing like the WT. wiry consistency. As the first team is at prment conatituted there are weak points, and if we n 1 the regular backs is off—l swert it fearlessly— there la not • aingle capable Mier, to fall back up in. Flow can the members be expected to bastovr their pa- tronage on such • product of mm s management and short sighted pulley P Importation, though it may seem • bugbear to the ufficials imbued with well • paramount idea of the excellence of local talent, is the only remedy, and wrely the cluh la wealthy enough to follow the ...- ample set it by poorer neighbours such as Widnes. A really class centre, half back, and wing, are badly needed in the premier organisation. There is • lot it wnw too in what your con rewunifent mays about Warrington heMg the happy hunting ground," of Widnes, it. ll,lew,Wigan and other clubs. What talent there le being f petered In the town Is calmly ellowed to be diverted by those more wideawake neighbours of ours butane,s were men- tinned in the comment netts...lame subject by the writer of ••Notes on tianw. and Athletics. in the ..E..miner." There in no °wanking the fact that more enterprise is essential on the governing hely, and 1111 k. It is early displayed the club which has borne • nsum second to none in Lancashire, will be held up as an ...ample of what out-of-date methods always lead to. I hope that the 'Alicia!s will take heed while there is yet time.— your.. etc, OVAL.</p>
