        <head>ilOrR OF E•III.IMTOWN. LODOR.</head>
        <p>ilOrR OF E•III.IMTOWN. LODOR.</p>
        <p>The Liana' weekly oeseion was hall on Monday night, presided over by Bro. T. Cutler, C.T. I brie were ...fenced to membership and several propoesel. Bro. E. Newman had received • latter from Bro. 0. Clarke thanking them for their kindness shown to hito,stating he was also improving in health. The re- port of the arrangements for New Vear's annual social wan adopted. Various letters and reports were then real anti dealt with.l t was decided to visit the Leigh Alpha Lodge in January, ant give them an entertainment. The Lodge wan then opened to the public. Bro. J. Warburton presiding ; and the fol- lowing took part. W. Willis, piano solo ;J. Single- ton, song ; T. William., reading ;J. Davies, mouth. organ solo ; .1. Taylor, song , J. liuddleston, siong. (encored) ; J Warburton, recitation. An interesting evening cloned with thank. to those who took part. " mussy Ten On Monday morning the decomposed body of John Marshall, better known as "Johnny the Haymaker," .2 years of age, of ni filed abode, was taken from the Sankey Canal near the Hay lock, Earleatown. The deceased, who wen one of Barbie- town's " characters.' had not been seen alive since the 240 h November, and on that date he left the Vulcan Inn, kept by Mr. E. L. Bent, saying that he was going to Bent's farm adj iining the flay lock, , to sleep for the night, and that he was going to lianyon on the fallowing day t, seek work. The body vont loon 1 In the lack by Thomas Screetch, lock keeper. who gave information to the police at V o'clock on Monday morning. —P.C. Schofield had the body remove Ito the District Council huildini on the Market ground. There were no marks of vi dem, or any suspicion of foul play. It Be sup- posed dec.oieed fell into the canal an the night was dark and foggy.—An inquest on the body was held at the New Market ItithEariellioww on Wednesday, by Mr.F.A. Jones, deputy ceroner,when evidence in accordance with the foregoing was tendereil. A verdict of " Found drowned wan returned. _Pal DR OP ISAILLIWITOWN TONTIng Society.</p>
        <p>The .venth annuli! general meeting of the above Society wa• held on Saturday evening at the Vi.- duct Hotel,Earleatown. The Secretary,Mr. C. Akid- ley,in euhmitting the account of the year'. work-1 ing,said he was artery that he wae not in a position to give the member. a substantial dividend aa in former year. owing to the very heavy demands on the fund. for sickness ant death,., of course they could ant hare it both ways,but no the whole they had had is Very eucceseful year being able to poky their way and hare a rmall balance ill hand. The total receipts far the year,arnount Mg to £7ll 10. A.. were distributed im follow.: -.C54 4r. 101. in pica pay, £5 through the death at ode member, £l4 13..24. for management awl other working Im- p... Jeering • balance in hand of /2 12r. 74.1. which it was unanimously resolved to carry for- ward to the rimerve fund,making the total nmeree £52 17s. 111. The election of nit :ere was then pro- ceeded with for fleet year. Mr. A. Oreatholder I elected chiormen,Mr. J. Shatp,treatturer, and Mr. C. Andle),secretary, re. prtmeetling. Ci 04.1 after an lenjoyable evening had been .1.. t. NrertoN•Lt•WILLOIVII C11011•L •nr, ORCHICSikAL</p>
        <p>The fifth concert given un ter the suapice• of the above society tsok place in t he Town If all,Newtoc. on flursday evening, before a fairly large audience. The reef/IMO:IMO CO/MUM OI a Cantata cf. teCiiia.,l Day 'by Van Bre.. For the leading pert Lee Louise Costna, of Chester, had been engnued. and she acquitted ',emelt admirably in the difficult task allotted her. The, choruses were also capitally ren- dered, the voices being nicely blended and excellent time was maintained throughout. Mr. F. H. Crowe ley wielded the baton, not Mr. H. Leyland officiated so accompsnia', The other item. on the programme reminded of an overture. " If erioneite," by the orchestra: violin solo, "Caret...a." If eVil. Crossley ; march and chorus, " Tannh..., 'sang," Valuta.. song." Mies Udine ;solo violin. Mr. r 11. Crossley; song, " The song of Hybris.. the Cretan," Mr. Col- -1 hugwood part song The Parting Ineh" diet" I ( would that my 15.," N 114.1 Appleton and Mr. Urge. ( hart ; dance " !Shepherd's dance," orcheetra ; giee I.' Dim Lice." BIRD AND POULTRY SHOW 17 Esnimirrown.</p>
        <p>The lire open show of poultry, pigeon, and rev birds in connection with tbe 1.:m.1,40w., Newton , land District Fancier's Akaociation was held in the Sltssion Hall, Carleetown, on Saturday. Every effort I was made by tlte committee P.) make thim, th Brat ' show, attractive and successful. The Motown Hall being well lighted and swim., is excellently mlapted for the awing of ' birds to advantage. As a Bret show it was eminently succeeeful there being between :Oland SOO entries in all the classes, exhibit. beta., sent from Holton, Manchester, Liverpool, Wigan, Warrington it, There was a good atten- dimes of the general public and others intere.ecl in the chow of piultry and birds. Seine excellent Ppeciinen• were on Pxhantion. There was plenty of competition in both fancy pigeons and working homers, and the exhibition. I were of thigh order. The judo,. were: - Poultry 1 and pigeon.. Mr. W. H. Bradley, II indley : cage ' birds : Mr. S. Taylor, Prescot. The whole of the arrangements were carried out by the committee, flews. Widd,Hallett,Payne, Sharp, Dickens, limit, Smith. Caldwell, Appleton. Boardman, Iltbsert and Lytbgoe secretary.Mr.W Bray, anti Jnial•Lant .oM- tar., Mr. S. Dicke... I 1111.10•1111.--Ganravun Iron-connwr, lisrrinenn. PICTURI IMPOKS.IN in anon, 41.11.7)1r. Pr none. LAUIIII' COMPANION., .I,n, O. Gaza, noulnollne. La— 31 Indio, on. nlinl9lll. tnklrir CROWS sad rhine. nr,4l. j In boank—Janz B. 611.11. Maslow, VI, Book.sees.</p>
